                <pb facs="0486" n="372" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg372"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/5506: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-09-10T16:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 10,
                                1924—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>151. Your 226, September 9, 5 p.m.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d324-f1"/> The
                    Department’s chief concern in the disturbance in China has been to afford
                    adequate protection to American life and property and to maintain an attitude of
                    strict neutrality. There is no thought of any other course of action and it is
                    assumed that the other principal Powers entertain similar views. There has been
                    no interchange of views with any of the Powers whose interests might be affected
                    by the developments in China. For your information there is quoted below a copy
                    of a telegram sent to Peking on September 8:</p>
                <p>[Here follows text of telegram no. 214, printed on <ref target="#pg371">page
                        371</ref>.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
